<BaseLayout
  title="How to Apply for a Tamworth Private Hire Driver Licence"
  description="How to apply for a Tamworth private hire driver licence: age 21+, enhanced DBS, the five-part knowledge test, Equo training, Blue Lamp driving assessment."
>
  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>How to Apply for a Tamworth Private Hire Driver Licence</h1>

      <p>
        <strong>Last checked: October 2026.</strong> Requirements and fees can
        change. Always check
        <a href="https://www.tamworth.gov.uk/licensing/taxis-and-private-hire" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >Tamworth Borough Council's official taxi and private hire pages</a
        >
        before you apply or pay anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Eligibility</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You must be <strong>over 21 years of age</strong>.</li>
        <li>You must have held a full UK driving licence (or the European equivalent) for <strong>at least 1 year</strong>.</li>
        <li>The council must judge you a <strong>fit and proper person</strong>. Its own test asks whether you would let your son or daughter, spouse or partner, parent, grandchild, or anyone else you care for, get into a vehicle with you alone.</li>
        <li>You must have the <strong>right to work</strong> in the UK.</li>
        <li>Tamworth <strong>does not currently licence Uber</strong>. If you want to drive for Uber, you would need to apply to a council that does, such as Wolverhampton.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Licence lengths:</strong> driver badges are issued for <strong>3 years</strong>. Vehicle licences are issued for one year.</p>
      <h2>Have these ready before you apply</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A <strong>recent digital photo</strong> you can upload: in colour, in focus, plain light background, facing the camera with a plain expression, eyes open and visible, no sunglasses or tinted glasses, nothing covering your face (a head covering is allowed for religious or medical reasons). Renewals need a new photo only if your appearance has changed.</li>
        <li>Your <strong>DVLA check code</strong>: eight digits, from gov.uk/view-driving-licence, using your 16-character driver number (field 5 on your photocard). Your driving record is checked every 12 months.</li>
        <li>Your driving licence number and expiry date (fields 5 and 4b).</li>
        <li>Your <strong>National Insurance number</strong>.</li>
        <li>Your <strong>last five years' residential history</strong>. If you have lived outside the UK for six months or more in that time, you will be asked to bring a certificate of good character from the relevant country or embassy after you apply.</li>
        <li>Your <strong>right to work</strong>: a right to work code from the Home Office, or an <strong>original</strong> document. You don't upload it, but you bring it to Marmion House after you apply. <strong>Photocopies are not accepted.</strong> Even UK citizens must show this.</li>
        <li>A <strong>credit or debit card</strong>. Tamworth does not accept cash.</li>
        <li>A <strong>tax check code</strong> (9 characters) if you are renewing, or if you have held a similar licence in the last 12 months. The check is on gov.uk.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Step by step</h2>
      <p>The council doesn't number its steps. This order follows its guidance.</p>
      <h3>1. Get your enhanced DBS check</h3>
      <p>If you need a new enhanced DBS, email <strong><a href="mailto:taxi-licensing@tamworth.gov.uk">taxi-licensing@tamworth.gov.uk</a></strong>. You receive an invite link from <strong>U Check</strong> (check your spam folder), so make sure the council has your correct email.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You pay the DBS fee to U Check when you enter your details.</li>
        <li>You can pay extra for a <strong>digital ID check</strong>, done online, so you don't have to go to the council offices. If you opt out, you are sent an appointment to show your documents to an officer.</li>
        <li>You provide ID such as a passport and driving licence, and a "selfie".</li>
        <li>When your certificate arrives, the licensing team must <strong>see the original</strong>.</li>
        <li>You have <strong>30 days from the date the certificate was issued</strong> to register for the <strong>DBS Update Service</strong>. There is an annual fee, and you must keep it paid. If you change your card, tell the Update Service, or the subscription may lapse.</li>
        <li>The council checks your DBS every <strong>6 months</strong>. If you aren't on the Update Service, or your circumstances change, you must get a new certificate and pay again.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>2. Submit your application online</h3>
      <p>Your application fee <strong>includes your first attempt at the knowledge test</strong>. Further attempts cost extra. Tamworth's fees are listed on the council's website, on the Environmental Health Fees &amp; Charges page, and <strong>no fee amounts are given in this guide</strong>.</p>
      <p>You are asked about any convictions, warnings, offences and cautions. If you declare any, the council contacts you to talk about them. You also sign a declaration that everything is true, and <strong>a false declaration is a criminal offence</strong>.</p>
      <h3>3. Medical</h3>
      <p>After you apply, the licensing team sends you a medical form. Any <strong>GP registered with the General Medical Council</strong> can complete it.</p>
      <p>It is repeated every <strong>5 years</strong> up to age 45, every <strong>3 years</strong> from 45 to 65, and <strong>every year</strong> from 65. Tamworth Licensing confirmed this in writing in October 2026.</p>
      <h3>4. Knowledge test</h3>
      <p>You are invited once your application has been accepted. The test is <strong>one hour with five sections, and you must pass all five</strong>:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Receipt</strong>: complete a receipt from a journey scenario. Only <strong>1 error</strong> is allowed.</li>
        <li><strong>English and arithmetic</strong>: 5 multiple choice, pass mark 3</li>
        <li><strong>Highway Code</strong>: 5 multiple choice, pass mark 3</li>
        <li><strong>Legislation and conditions</strong>: 10 questions, pass mark 8</li>
        <li><strong>Local knowledge</strong>: name the road for 10 places, pass mark 7</li>
      </ol>
      <p>It is done <strong>without translation</strong>. You get a maximum of <strong>3 attempts</strong>; after three failures, your application is refused and you can't reapply for <strong>12 months</strong>. Give at least <strong>2 clear working days'</strong> notice to cancel, or you pay the fee and it counts as a fail. Renewals do not sit the test.</p>
      <h3>5. Disability and safeguarding training</h3>
      <p>All new applicants, and existing drivers who want to renew, must complete <strong>online disability awareness and safeguarding modules every three years</strong>. The provider is <strong>Equo Training</strong> (equotraining.co.uk/tamworth). Click <strong>BUY NOW</strong> and pay by card. When you pass you get a certificate, which you email to the licensing team.</p>
      <h3>6. Driving assessment</h3>
      <p>New applicants only. You are told to book once your application has been accepted.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Book online, any time, with <strong>The Blue Lamp Trust</strong> (bltdrivertraining.co.uk).</li>
        <li>An assessor contacts you within <strong>48 hours (2 working days)</strong> to agree a date, time and place.</li>
        <li>You get the result <strong>straight away</strong>. You are allowed up to <strong>9 driving faults</strong>, and <strong>no serious or dangerous faults</strong>.</li>
        <li>If you pass, you get a marking sheet and a hard copy certificate, which you send to <a href="mailto:taxi-licensing@tamworth.gov.uk">taxi-licensing@tamworth.gov.uk</a>.</li>
        <li>Blue Lamp Trust: <a href="mailto:info@bltdrivertraining.co.uk">info@bltdrivertraining.co.uk</a>, 0330 4416 847.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>7. Licence issued</h3>
      <p>Once everything is complete, you are issued your licence and <strong>two badges</strong> (see below).</p>
      <h2>Once you are licensed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Two badges:</strong> wear one at all times, plainly visible. The second is displayed prominently in the vehicle.</li>
        <li><strong>DVLA check code:</strong> if the council asks for it, provide it within <strong>7 days</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Change of personal details</strong> (such as your address): tell the council in writing within <strong>5 working days</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Away from your registered address for more than 7 days:</strong> tell the council in advance, in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>Arrest, charge or conviction</strong> for a sexual offence, dishonesty, violence, or any motoring offence: tell the council within <strong>48 hours</strong>. Failing to report is treated as an honesty problem in its own right, even if you are later cleared.</li>
        <li><strong>Any other conviction or caution:</strong> within <strong>7 working days</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Accepting a fixed penalty notice:</strong> within <strong>5 working days</strong>, and again straight away once it is endorsed.</li>
        <li><strong>Illness or injury affecting your driving:</strong> within <strong>10 working days</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Penalty points:</strong> reaching <strong>12 points within 3 years</strong> means an officer reviews your record and will normally refer you to the Licensing Committee. It isn't automatic revocation, and you can make submissions and appeal to the Magistrates' Court.</li>
        <li><strong>Suspension, revocation, refusal to renew or expiry:</strong> return your badges and licence within <strong>7 days</strong>, or sooner if the council asks.</li>
      </ul>
